From db4875d87926998c0ef2e04ac8db0e9a7192b319 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Ville=20Kivela=CC=88?= <vkivela@hotmail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 12:12:51 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 09/13] extensions: answer webNavigation.getFrame and
 getAllFrames

A password manager's worker registers its message listener inside
asynchronous set-up, long after its script has evaluated, so the first
page's message finds no listener and is dropped, in Chrome as here.
Chrome users never notice, because the worker then reaches back into
every open tab: tabs.query, then webNavigation.getAllFrames for each,
then scripting.executeScript into every frame. With getAllFrames
unknown the callback never fires, the loop never injects, and the first
page after a launch has no autofill until it is reloaded.

Chrome answers these from a per-document navigation state that its
tab-strip observer maintains, which is the half of webNavigation the
research marked as a stop. It is not needed: the frame itself knows its
committed URL and whether it is an error document, and the core
ExtensionApiFrameIdMap numbers frames and documents. The two functions
read the tab off the registry and the frames off its WebContents. The
events stay declared and unraised.

Two fixtures with the worker shapes the existing ones lacked: one that
is busy for its first moments, whose message is parked and delivered,
and one that listens late and reaches back the way Bitwarden does.
